1
00:00:01,251 --> 00:00:03,253
[♪ western music plays]

2
00:00:04,838 --> 00:00:06,924
[Harold, in English]
Everywhere you turn in Monument Valley,

3
00:00:07,007 --> 00:00:08,383
it's always magical.

4
00:00:08,467 --> 00:00:13,472
When I go away, I always want to come back
and look at it again and so on.

5
00:00:19,144 --> 00:00:20,896
[narrator] It's the special summer break
Noel organized

6
00:00:20,979 --> 00:00:22,564
for the Travis Japan members.

7
00:00:23,941 --> 00:00:28,070
At the foot of the Rocky Mountains
where the desert breeze blows

8
00:00:28,153 --> 00:00:31,782
lies the real America
the members have yet to see.

9
00:00:34,576 --> 00:00:38,121
The Travis Japan members except for Noel
divide up into two groups

10
00:00:38,205 --> 00:00:40,165
and head to their final destination.
Sedona, Arizona.

11
00:00:42,501 --> 00:00:44,962
Noel sets out to Sedona on his own
before the others

12
00:00:45,045 --> 00:00:47,464
to quietly prepare
a warm welcome for them.

13
00:00:49,091 --> 00:00:51,218
[♪ opening theme music plays]

14
00:01:04,773 --> 00:01:07,234
Travis Japan
SUMMER VACATION!! IN THE USA

15
00:01:11,071 --> 00:01:12,406
[♪ rock music plays]

16
00:01:14,533 --> 00:01:15,701
Thanks for routing me.

17
00:01:15,784 --> 00:01:17,578
[narrator]
Picking up where we last left off,

18
00:01:17,661 --> 00:01:19,621
we follow Team A's Chaka, Umi, and Shizu

19
00:01:19,705 --> 00:01:22,583
as they travel through Monument Valley,
where the Navajo Nation,

20
00:01:22,666 --> 00:01:25,544
the largest Native American tribe
in the United States, resides.

21
00:01:26,545 --> 00:01:28,630
[in English] Monument Valley, Utah.

22
00:01:30,257 --> 00:01:32,092
[♪ rock music continues]

23
00:01:41,602 --> 00:01:43,770
[in Japanese]
They drive through the vast wilderness

24
00:01:43,854 --> 00:01:45,605
of this 337,000 square kilometer desert.

25
00:01:45,689 --> 00:01:51,194
The classic American landscape
seems to stretch endlessly.

26
00:01:59,286 --> 00:02:01,121
[♪ rock music stops]

27
00:02:01,204 --> 00:02:03,332
[Umi] There. Look, look. Look, it's tiny.

28
00:02:03,415 --> 00:02:06,043
-Oh, really?
-[Umi] See? It's a tiny place.

29
00:02:06,126 --> 00:02:08,670
You've gotta open your eyes
real wide to find it.

30
00:02:11,465 --> 00:02:14,217
We drive all the way down… And stop here.

31
00:02:14,301 --> 00:02:16,011
[Umi] Hold on. That's pretty far.

32
00:02:18,347 --> 00:02:21,350
[exclaims]

33
00:02:22,017 --> 00:02:23,560
It's the first Japanese food
we've had in a while.

34
00:02:23,644 --> 00:02:25,228
-[Shizu, in English] Okay. Let's go.
-Let's go!

35
00:02:25,312 --> 00:02:26,480
Let's go!

36
00:02:26,563 --> 00:02:28,732
[Shizu whoops]

37
00:02:29,483 --> 00:02:31,485
[♪ upbeat music plays]

38
00:02:48,335 --> 00:02:49,586
[♪ music continues]

39
00:02:53,507 --> 00:02:54,675
"Cameron".

40
00:02:54,758 --> 00:02:55,926
"Cameron".

41
00:02:56,009 --> 00:02:57,344
[Shizu, in Japanese] What, what?

42
00:02:57,427 --> 00:02:58,845
[in English] "Indian arts and…"

43
00:02:59,805 --> 00:03:01,640
[narrator, in Japanese]
This is a historical post

44
00:03:01,723 --> 00:03:03,350
with almost a century behind it.

45
00:03:03,433 --> 00:03:06,978
Originally used by traders to barter
goods with the Native American tribes,

46
00:03:07,062 --> 00:03:08,689
it now preserves
the indigenous traditions and culture

47
00:03:08,772 --> 00:03:10,524
by functioning as a living, historic site.

48
00:03:10,607 --> 00:03:12,651
-[Shizu, in English] Hi.
-Sorry, guys, I blew it.

49
00:03:12,734 --> 00:03:13,735
-Hey, nice to meet you.
-Hi.

50
00:03:13,819 --> 00:03:15,112
-Thank you.
-Very nice to meet you too.

51
00:03:15,195 --> 00:03:16,446
-I'm Chaka.
-Chaka?

52
00:03:16,530 --> 00:03:17,906
-I'm Umi. Yeah.
-Umi.

53
00:03:17,989 --> 00:03:19,157
-I'm Shizu.
-You're Shizu.

54
00:03:19,241 --> 00:03:20,867
-Very, very nice to meet you.
-Nice to meet you.

55
00:03:20,951 --> 00:03:23,078
Welcome. I'm glad you guys are here.

56
00:03:23,161 --> 00:03:25,163
-Thank you so much.
-A lot of the kids inside here

57
00:03:25,247 --> 00:03:28,041
-are very excited about you coming.
-Wow. Yay!

58
00:03:28,125 --> 00:03:29,668
-We are so excited.
-They were showing the picture

59
00:03:29,751 --> 00:03:31,253
and everything. So that's wonderful.

60
00:03:31,336 --> 00:03:32,879
So, before we go in,

61
00:03:32,963 --> 00:03:34,589
-can I show you something?
-Yeah.

62
00:03:34,673 --> 00:03:36,633
-Thank you.
-Let's go this way.

63
00:03:36,716 --> 00:03:37,968
[Shizu] We want to see that. Whoo!

64
00:03:38,051 --> 00:03:40,220
In 1934, the Navajo Nation

65
00:03:40,303 --> 00:03:43,640
was expanded
to include this particular property.

66
00:03:44,141 --> 00:03:46,435
So we're kind of like a little island of…

67
00:03:46,518 --> 00:03:48,311
All right, so, one more time.

68
00:03:48,395 --> 00:03:50,397
-[shop owner laughs]
-[Shizu] Right there is good.

69
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:53,984
Hubert Richardson built
the trading post back here in 1911,

70
00:03:54,067 --> 00:03:56,111
because they built this bridge.

71
00:03:56,194 --> 00:03:57,195
[Umi, Shizu, Chaka] Whoa.

72
00:03:57,279 --> 00:04:00,198
So this is the whole reason
why all of this is here.

73
00:04:00,866 --> 00:04:03,910
In the old days,
when this was built in 1911,

74
00:04:04,661 --> 00:04:06,455
The people that would come to trade here,

75
00:04:06,538 --> 00:04:12,586
sometimes they had to come
for days on-on wagons with, uh, horses.

76
00:04:12,669 --> 00:04:16,548
So at the time we had, uh, guest hogans
or guest homes

77
00:04:16,631 --> 00:04:18,550
that they could stay in for several days

78
00:04:18,633 --> 00:04:21,094
while they completed their trade
at the trading post.

79
00:04:21,178 --> 00:04:24,431
And the old traders,
they weren't just traders,

80
00:04:24,514 --> 00:04:30,395
they weren't just trading merchandise
and, uh, livestock for dry goods.

81
00:04:30,479 --> 00:04:35,358
They were also kind of like liaisons
for their culture, for the United States.

82
00:04:36,485 --> 00:04:38,653
Things were very, very different out here.

83
00:04:38,737 --> 00:04:42,365
Um, and a lot of times,
the local people didn't understand

84
00:04:42,449 --> 00:04:43,909
what they were looking at.

85
00:04:43,992 --> 00:04:48,789
So the trader would interpret letters,
papers and help them in that way.

86
00:04:48,872 --> 00:04:51,500
I'll show you
the main building over there. The…

87
00:04:51,583 --> 00:04:53,502
This was the old motel building

88
00:04:53,585 --> 00:04:57,464
but we've turned it into
an antique Native American arts gallery.

89
00:04:57,547 --> 00:04:59,007
-Can we see? [cheers]
-Yeah, of course.

90
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:01,802
-I would love to. It would be my pleasure.
-[Shizu, in Japanese] They sell antiques.

91
00:05:10,602 --> 00:05:11,603
[Umi, in English] Thank you.

92
00:05:11,686 --> 00:05:13,438
So that's what we have over here.

93
00:05:13,522 --> 00:05:16,650
We have a mixture of the contemporary art,

94
00:05:16,733 --> 00:05:19,611
the really fine contemporary
Native American art,

95
00:05:19,694 --> 00:05:25,200
as well as, uh, the antiques that they
actually made and used themselves,

96
00:05:25,283 --> 00:05:27,285
you know, 100 years ago.

97
00:05:27,369 --> 00:05:30,497
And then over the winter, uh,
destroyed all their…

98
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:32,207
These were all handmade.

99
00:05:32,290 --> 00:05:33,708
-[Umi] All handmade?
-Mm-hmm.

100
00:05:34,417 --> 00:05:37,671
And then they were made
for themselves like this one.

101
00:05:37,754 --> 00:05:39,965
But sometimes they also made
them like this one,

102
00:05:40,048 --> 00:05:44,177
that would be for trade when
the railroads came through around 1890.

103
00:05:44,261 --> 00:05:47,931
But sometime around 1930 to 1940,

104
00:05:48,431 --> 00:05:52,435
the knowledge of how to make these
was lost.

105
00:05:53,728 --> 00:05:56,648
And, so,
there will be no more of these baskets.

106
00:05:56,731 --> 00:05:59,276
-No one remembers how to make them.
-No more?

107
00:05:59,359 --> 00:06:03,905
-And that blanket runs $250,000.
-[Chaka] What?

108
00:06:03,989 --> 00:06:04,990
-[Shizu] What? Huh?
-[Umi] Wait.

109
00:06:05,073 --> 00:06:06,491
How much is it that in yen?

110
00:06:06,575 --> 00:06:10,036
[Chaka]
One, ten, 100, 1,000, 10,000, 100,000…

111
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:11,371
-Four?
-About a whopping 40 million yen.

112
00:06:11,454 --> 00:06:12,998
-Oh, by far.
-Holy mackerel!

113
00:06:13,081 --> 00:06:14,666
-That's crazy expensive.
-[shop owner] It's a lot.

114
00:06:14,749 --> 00:06:16,543
-He can… He can buy.
-[shop owner laughs]

115
00:06:16,626 --> 00:06:17,836
-[shop owner] He can buy it?
-No, no.

116
00:06:17,919 --> 00:06:19,379
-Hey.
-[Umi] He has a…

117
00:06:19,462 --> 00:06:20,463
[Shizu, shop owner laugh]

118
00:06:21,715 --> 00:06:22,841
[Umi] Wow.

119
00:06:26,511 --> 00:06:29,931
-[Shizu, in Japanese] Wow, look at it.
-Cool! Wow. I love it.

120
00:06:30,015 --> 00:06:31,474
Hey, wait…

121
00:06:31,558 --> 00:06:34,477
[shop owner, in English] The value
of this weaving is really intrinsic.

122
00:06:35,061 --> 00:06:39,900
The value of the weaving
is its history being… having been woven.

123
00:06:39,983 --> 00:06:43,403
The United States government
was trying to change their culture.

124
00:06:43,486 --> 00:06:47,532
They were trying to turn them from
Native Americans into white farmers.

125
00:06:48,116 --> 00:06:52,495
And this Navajo woman,
when she was at Bosque Redondo,

126
00:06:52,579 --> 00:06:56,082
I don't know how hard the march was
for her but I know it was hard.

127
00:06:56,625 --> 00:06:59,794
To me, this is her way of saying,

128
00:06:59,878 --> 00:07:02,547
-"No. I'm not going to change."
-[Chaka] Yeah.

129
00:07:02,631 --> 00:07:03,923
"This is who I am.

130
00:07:04,591 --> 00:07:06,593
And I'm gonna weave this here."

131
00:07:06,676 --> 00:07:09,262
[Chaka] This seem to say a lot of things.

132
00:07:09,346 --> 00:07:14,517
That's… that's pain, that's brave,
a lot of things.

133
00:07:14,601 --> 00:07:15,935
That's exactly right.

134
00:07:17,187 --> 00:07:18,688
I'm glad to hear you say that.

135
00:07:18,772 --> 00:07:21,566
A lot of people don't really understand.

136
00:07:22,067 --> 00:07:24,694
[Noel, in Japanese] Through hearing the
rich stories about this region's people

137
00:07:25,195 --> 00:07:26,196
from locals like him,

138
00:07:26,279 --> 00:07:29,115
I've come to see
that the decorations and accessories

139
00:07:29,199 --> 00:07:31,326
we often regard as purely aesthetic

140
00:07:31,409 --> 00:07:33,578
are deeply rooted in culture and history.

141
00:07:42,462 --> 00:07:44,631
It suits you. The headpiece
complements your facial features.

142
00:07:45,382 --> 00:07:46,883
Yeah, it's a perfect fit.

143
00:07:46,966 --> 00:07:48,134
[shop owner] These are dream catchers.

144
00:07:48,218 --> 00:07:52,055
The design itself is probably from
the Plains Indians…

145
00:07:52,138 --> 00:07:54,599
-[Chaka] Mm-hmm.
-…but the tradition is that

146
00:07:54,683 --> 00:08:00,271
these are a web that catches
your nightmares, your bad dreams.

147
00:08:00,355 --> 00:08:01,398
-Uh-huh.
-So at night,

148
00:08:01,481 --> 00:08:03,775
your bad dreams,
they get caught in this web…

149
00:08:03,858 --> 00:08:06,569
-I see. Yeah.
-…and they can't get to your head.

150
00:08:06,653 --> 00:08:08,154
-Uh-huh.
-And then in the morning,

151
00:08:08,238 --> 00:08:10,448
the sun comes and washes them away.

152
00:08:10,532 --> 00:08:11,741
-Uh-huh.
-But your good dreams

153
00:08:11,825 --> 00:08:13,785
-are able to get through.
-Oh, I see that.

154
00:08:13,868 --> 00:08:16,121
-This is pretty cool.
-It's a catch for the bad dream.

155
00:08:16,705 --> 00:08:19,833
Yes. Catches the bad dreams,
they get caught in the net.

156
00:08:20,333 --> 00:08:23,461
The good dreams go around the
sides and can come through the bottom.

157
00:08:23,545 --> 00:08:26,214
-Oh, I think that's here. Uh-huh.
-Yes.

158
00:08:26,297 --> 00:08:27,590
-Yeah.
-Only a good dream.

159
00:08:27,674 --> 00:08:28,675
-Yes.
-I see now.

160
00:08:28,758 --> 00:08:30,552
-Anywhere in your bedroom is fine.
-Uh-huh.

161
00:08:30,635 --> 00:08:31,636
Yeah.

162
00:08:35,890 --> 00:08:38,810
[shop owner] So these are also made
by the Diné, or the Navajo.

163
00:08:38,893 --> 00:08:41,938
-[Umi] Yep.
-Each one of these pieces is handmade.

164
00:08:42,021 --> 00:08:44,065
[Umi] Handmade.
Oh, Native American handmade.

165
00:08:44,149 --> 00:08:46,985
Mm-hmm. Now the pot itself is a ceramic.

166
00:08:47,068 --> 00:08:49,320
It's kind of like the, uh, uh…

167
00:08:49,404 --> 00:08:51,990
-the canvas for a painting.
-Uh-huh.

168
00:08:52,073 --> 00:08:55,368
So they take this pot,
and then they color it,

169
00:08:55,452 --> 00:08:58,538
and then they etch each one
of these tiny lines by hand.

170
00:08:58,621 --> 00:08:59,622
Whoa.

171
00:08:59,706 --> 00:09:03,543
We work with a family
of Navajo pottery artists.

172
00:09:03,626 --> 00:09:08,548
So it's a family thing that's passed on
from mother to daughter to daughter.

173
00:09:08,631 --> 00:09:10,550
Mm-hmm. Whoa.

174
00:09:10,633 --> 00:09:12,093
It is so…

175
00:09:13,011 --> 00:09:15,847
Generation… Over the generations.

176
00:09:15,930 --> 00:09:17,182
Exactly.

177
00:09:17,265 --> 00:09:18,975
-Yes.
-Whoa.

178
00:09:19,059 --> 00:09:22,312
-Most Native American art is like that.
-Mmm.

179
00:09:22,395 --> 00:09:26,399
You don't go to school for four years
or eight years to learn how to do it.

180
00:09:26,483 --> 00:09:28,943
-Oh, yes.
-You work with your grandparents

181
00:09:29,027 --> 00:09:32,530
and your parents
and other artists in the area.

182
00:09:33,198 --> 00:09:35,450
That's how you learn it.
Through apprenticeship.

183
00:09:39,162 --> 00:09:43,249
So these are also another
Navajo or Diné tradition.

184
00:09:43,333 --> 00:09:45,335
Um, these juniper berries here…

185
00:09:47,670 --> 00:09:49,964
these are the seeds from the juniper tree.

186
00:09:50,048 --> 00:09:51,716
-Mm-hmm.
-Kind of a conifer.

187
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:56,554
They fall on the ground
and then the ants will eat them hollow.

188
00:09:56,638 --> 00:09:59,307
And the Navajo, they pick these up

189
00:09:59,974 --> 00:10:03,144
and they pierce the other end
to make them into beads.

190
00:10:03,228 --> 00:10:05,021
And the tradition is,

191
00:10:06,356 --> 00:10:08,191
this protects you

192
00:10:08,733 --> 00:10:12,946
from, uh, people who have bad intentions
or are trying to bad things to you

193
00:10:13,029 --> 00:10:14,948
or just are not nice people.

194
00:10:15,031 --> 00:10:16,658
It protects you from them.

195
00:10:16,741 --> 00:10:18,159
Keeps that away.

196
00:10:18,785 --> 00:10:20,703
They call them, uh, cedar beads.

197
00:10:20,787 --> 00:10:23,581
Sometimes they call them
ghost beads as well.

198
00:10:23,665 --> 00:10:24,958
So cool.

199
00:10:26,584 --> 00:10:29,003
[Chaka, in Japanese] He explained
a different item to each of us.

200
00:10:29,087 --> 00:10:30,839
[Umi] About the ceramics
and whatnot, yep, too.

201
00:10:32,257 --> 00:10:33,675
You should bring it back with you.

202
00:10:33,758 --> 00:10:35,260
How about… this
will be my souvenir for Kura.

203
00:10:35,343 --> 00:10:37,053
-Good idea, it's right down his alley.
-It's his style.

204
00:10:37,137 --> 00:10:40,348
Then I'll grab something for Shime…
Or Genta? Which one?

205
00:10:40,431 --> 00:10:42,433
-I'm claiming the dream catcher.
-Well, yeah, but…

206
00:10:42,517 --> 00:10:45,145
Okay, you get something for Shime.
I'll get Genta a dream catcher, yeah.

207
00:10:45,228 --> 00:10:47,939
-Then we'll decide the souvenir for Noel.
-We'll all decide, the three of us.

208
00:10:48,022 --> 00:10:49,566
Okay. Got it. Bye-bye!

209
00:10:49,649 --> 00:10:52,360
It matches the decorations at his house.

210
00:10:52,443 --> 00:10:53,862
It has blue in it.

211
00:10:54,362 --> 00:10:57,949
It'll catch all his nightmares
so that only the good dreams can pass.

212
00:10:59,951 --> 00:11:00,952
[laughs]

213
00:11:04,539 --> 00:11:05,999
[♪ upbeat music plays]

214
00:11:11,462 --> 00:11:12,672
Uh…

215
00:11:14,048 --> 00:11:16,009
Oh, look. I like this one. It's cute.

216
00:11:16,885 --> 00:11:17,886
I choose this one.

217
00:11:21,139 --> 00:11:22,140
This one.

218
00:11:22,223 --> 00:11:24,475
[Chaka] Oh, 100%.

219
00:11:24,559 --> 00:11:26,102
It's precious.

220
00:11:26,728 --> 00:11:27,770
What do you think?

221
00:11:29,147 --> 00:11:31,316
-Yep, this one.
-[laughs]

222
00:11:31,399 --> 00:11:33,359
It's a done deal. This one's for Shime.

223
00:11:34,485 --> 00:11:35,987
[♪ music continues]

224
00:11:42,285 --> 00:11:43,453
Oh, how about this one?

225
00:11:44,245 --> 00:11:45,246
[Shizu] A cat.

226
00:11:45,330 --> 00:11:46,831
-[Chaka] How nice. Kitty-cat.
-Look, a cat statue.

227
00:11:47,540 --> 00:11:49,167
-It's nice. But it's…
-And it's turquoise.

228
00:11:49,250 --> 00:11:51,044
-Perfect then.
-It's got nothing to do with

229
00:11:51,127 --> 00:11:52,629
the Native culture, but, oh, well.

230
00:11:52,712 --> 00:11:54,464
-Well, I suppose…
-Yeah.

231
00:11:54,547 --> 00:11:57,884
…it's one of the ceramic cats for Noel.

232
00:11:57,967 --> 00:11:59,761
-This one it is. Meow, meow.
-Oh, and Noel has two cats.

233
00:11:59,844 --> 00:12:01,846
-And this one, too.
-Noel has two cats, this is perfect.

234
00:12:01,930 --> 00:12:03,097
We've got a pair of cats.

235
00:12:03,181 --> 00:12:04,807
-They're symmetrical.
-[Shizu] Symmetrical!

236
00:12:04,891 --> 00:12:06,267
Meow, meow.

237
00:12:09,896 --> 00:12:11,231
[Genta] Over here?

238
00:12:13,066 --> 00:12:14,234
-How early.
-It's open already.

239
00:12:14,317 --> 00:12:17,028
-They open at 6:30.
-Thanks.

240
00:12:17,737 --> 00:12:19,072
Americano?

241
00:12:19,155 --> 00:12:20,156
-[barista] Americano?
-Yeah.

242
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:21,449
[barista] Do you want that iced or hot?

243
00:12:21,532 --> 00:12:23,493
-Uh, Ice. Ice, please.
-[barista] Great.

244
00:12:23,576 --> 00:12:25,578
-Thanks.
-Thank you so much.

245
00:12:25,662 --> 00:12:26,663
Thank you.

246
00:12:27,830 --> 00:12:30,124
[in Japanese] Oh.
Ready for today's message?

247
00:12:30,208 --> 00:12:31,209
Ready.

248
00:12:31,292 --> 00:12:35,713
"Today, you are visiting
a historic railroad town."

249
00:12:35,797 --> 00:12:37,215
-Railroad?
-Railroad.

250
00:12:37,298 --> 00:12:41,261
"But you won't be boarding
the locomotive until tomorrow.

251
00:12:41,344 --> 00:12:43,346
Enjoy exploring the area today."

252
00:12:43,846 --> 00:12:45,515
And, "Just a heads-up.

253
00:12:45,598 --> 00:12:46,724
-Genta…
-Yes.

254
00:12:46,808 --> 00:12:48,059
…know when to stop."

255
00:12:48,726 --> 00:12:50,186
Well, you heard.

256
00:12:50,853 --> 00:12:52,230
What is he talking about?

257
00:12:52,313 --> 00:12:53,898
A ride on the locomotive…
Oh, that's tomorrow.

258
00:12:53,982 --> 00:12:55,650
-Yep, tomorrow.
-We're boarding a train tomorrow.

259
00:12:55,733 --> 00:12:57,068
-But for today, we're only sightseeing?
-Thank you.

260
00:12:57,151 --> 00:13:00,321
-Sightseeing?
-Oh, do we have a free day?

261
00:13:00,405 --> 00:13:03,491
I think that's the idea, yeah.
"Genta, know when to stop."

262
00:13:03,574 --> 00:13:06,119
Sightseeing today, locomotive tomorrow.

263
00:13:06,202 --> 00:13:07,870
-Genta, know when to stop.
-Genta, know when to stop.

264
00:13:07,954 --> 00:13:09,664
[Genta] Hey now.

265
00:13:09,747 --> 00:13:11,082
Just…

266
00:13:11,165 --> 00:13:12,166
[Genta] What?

267
00:13:12,250 --> 00:13:14,043
There's no way to tell
what he's referring to at this stage.

268
00:13:14,127 --> 00:13:16,546
[Machu] It's like Noel prepared riddles
for us to solve.

269
00:13:16,629 --> 00:13:18,840
-[Genta laughs]
-He's toying with us.

270
00:13:18,923 --> 00:13:20,258
-He's messing with us.
-Is that what that is?

271
00:13:20,341 --> 00:13:21,926
He got us good.

272
00:13:22,010 --> 00:13:23,886
[Machu] It's as if he's daring us
to crack a puzzle.

273
00:13:23,970 --> 00:13:25,763
-Only way to find out is to start.
-He's giving us riddles?

274
00:13:25,847 --> 00:13:27,765
-Well, if we walk around…
-Walk around, and…

275
00:13:27,849 --> 00:13:29,642
Let's explore the town as we go along.

276
00:13:29,726 --> 00:13:31,269
-Shall we?
-Shime, your…

277
00:13:31,352 --> 00:13:32,395
Yeah.

278
00:13:32,478 --> 00:13:33,646
Do you wanna finish it,

279
00:13:33,730 --> 00:13:35,398
-or ask for a doggie bag?
-I'll get a bag.

280
00:13:35,481 --> 00:13:37,608
-Yep.
-[in English] Excuse me.

281
00:13:38,735 --> 00:13:41,070
Uh, I want a to-go? Takeout?

282
00:13:41,154 --> 00:13:42,697
-[barista] Have a great day.
-Yeah. Thank you.

283
00:13:42,780 --> 00:13:43,781
Thank you so much.

284
00:13:44,991 --> 00:13:46,451
[in Japanese] Time to explore the town.

285
00:13:54,584 --> 00:13:55,918
Bread… Bread. Bread's delicious.

286
00:13:56,002 --> 00:14:00,506
I've always wanted to commute
with a coffee in one hand.

287
00:14:00,590 --> 00:14:02,425
-[Genta] It came true.
-Good for you.

288
00:14:02,508 --> 00:14:05,094
Seriously though.
What did Noel mean, "Know when to stop?"

289
00:14:05,178 --> 00:14:06,971
-[Machu] Yeah, now I'm curious.
-[Genta] Hmm.

290
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:08,681
[Machu] Mind if I start driving?

291
00:14:08,765 --> 00:14:10,058
-[Shime] Go.
-[Machu] All righty.

292
00:14:10,141 --> 00:14:11,392
[Shime, in English] Let's go!

293
00:14:11,476 --> 00:14:13,352
-[Genta] Let's go. Let's go.
-[Shime] Let's go.

294
00:14:13,936 --> 00:14:15,646
[Shime, in Japanese]
You're getting the hang of driving.

295
00:14:15,730 --> 00:14:16,814
[Machu] Am I?

296
00:14:17,523 --> 00:14:19,108
[Genta] You look cool.

297
00:14:21,694 --> 00:14:23,696
-Give us a shout, Shime.
-[in English] Let's go!

298
00:14:23,780 --> 00:14:24,864
[Genta laughs]

299
00:14:24,947 --> 00:14:26,824
[Machu, in Japanese] It's the best!

300
00:14:26,908 --> 00:14:29,660
[all laugh, cheer]

301
00:14:32,955 --> 00:14:34,457
[♪ blues music plays]

302
00:14:42,173 --> 00:14:44,175
[♪ music continues]

303
00:14:57,146 --> 00:15:00,775
[Noel] I'm in Colorado,
the state with the highest altitude.

304
00:15:02,777 --> 00:15:05,113
The people reside in a city
nested in the Rocky Mountains,

305
00:15:05,196 --> 00:15:07,156
which reaches 4,000 meters in elevation.

306
00:15:08,741 --> 00:15:13,329
To the west, tucked away
in the rugged San Juan Mountains

307
00:15:13,412 --> 00:15:15,456
is a tiny town called Durango.

308
00:15:18,793 --> 00:15:21,003
[narrator, in English] Durango, Colorado.

309
00:15:30,179 --> 00:15:32,181
[train whistle blows]

310
00:15:32,265 --> 00:15:36,394
[narrator, in Japanese] Founded in 1881,
Durango began as a railroad town built

311
00:15:36,477 --> 00:15:40,314
to serve the gold and silver mines
of the San Juan.

312
00:15:41,315 --> 00:15:48,322
The town is dotted with Victorian-era
buildings reminiscent of the Old Frontier.

313
00:15:48,406 --> 00:15:50,158
[Machu] Here we go, here we go!

314
00:15:50,241 --> 00:15:51,576
[Genta] We're here!

315
00:15:51,659 --> 00:15:54,203
-Yeah!
-Yeah!

316
00:15:54,287 --> 00:15:55,788
[♪ upbeat music plays]

317
00:16:20,897 --> 00:16:22,398
[♪ music continues]

318
00:16:49,300 --> 00:16:50,676
What a fancy building.

319
00:16:50,760 --> 00:16:51,761
Oh, super fancy.

320
00:16:54,388 --> 00:16:55,723
[Machu laughs]

321
00:16:56,515 --> 00:16:57,516
Ready?

322
00:16:58,309 --> 00:16:59,352
Say cheese.

323
00:17:00,269 --> 00:17:01,270
[camera shutter clicks]

324
00:17:01,354 --> 00:17:02,605
[Genta laughs]

325
00:17:02,688 --> 00:17:03,981
[Shime laughs]

326
00:17:04,065 --> 00:17:05,274
[Machu] Great.

327
00:17:06,442 --> 00:17:07,652
Say cheese.

328
00:17:07,735 --> 00:17:08,903
[camera shutter clicks]

329
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:24,168
[camera shutter clicks]

330
00:17:25,086 --> 00:17:26,170
[♪ music stops]

331
00:17:26,254 --> 00:17:27,380
This is the hotel we're staying at.

332
00:17:27,463 --> 00:17:28,923
-Amazing.
-Wow!

333
00:17:29,006 --> 00:17:30,299
Let's go check in.

334
00:17:30,841 --> 00:17:33,386
[Shime] Wow, what a gorgeous hotel.

335
00:17:33,970 --> 00:17:35,429
[narrator] This hotel
has provided beds for travelers

336
00:17:35,513 --> 00:17:36,681
since the days of the Old Frontier.

337
00:17:36,764 --> 00:17:37,765
We'll be immersed in history.

338
00:17:37,848 --> 00:17:39,600
-Right?
-[receptionist, in English] Hi.

339
00:17:39,684 --> 00:17:40,685
-Hi.
-Hello.

340
00:17:40,768 --> 00:17:42,395
[narrator, in Japanese]
From its antique interiors

341
00:17:42,478 --> 00:17:43,854
to the hotel staff's period uniforms,

342
00:17:43,938 --> 00:17:46,399
the hotel feels like
a window into the bygone days.

343
00:17:47,024 --> 00:17:49,235
-Do you like it? Yes.
-[Machu] Yeah, of course.

344
00:17:49,318 --> 00:17:50,444
We like it.

345
00:17:50,528 --> 00:17:51,612
It's pretty cool.

346
00:17:51,696 --> 00:17:52,697
[Genta] Yes.

347
00:17:52,780 --> 00:17:55,324
So we opened our doors in 1887.

348
00:17:55,408 --> 00:17:56,576
-Mmm.
-1887.

349
00:17:56,659 --> 00:17:59,203
[receptionist] Yeah, we're the second
oldest business in Durango.

350
00:17:59,287 --> 00:18:00,871
The first one being the railroad.

351
00:18:00,955 --> 00:18:02,206
-[Shime] Wow. Cool.
-Yeah.

352
00:18:02,290 --> 00:18:04,375
-So we've been around for a little while.
-[all laugh]

353
00:18:04,458 --> 00:18:05,459
[Genta] Yeah.

354
00:18:06,002 --> 00:18:07,837
[receptionist]
So your room number is right here.

355
00:18:07,920 --> 00:18:08,921
[Machu] Okay.

356
00:18:09,005 --> 00:18:11,090
[receptionist]
And I'm just gonna hand you these then.

357
00:18:11,173 --> 00:18:12,174
-Thanks so much.
-Thank you!

358
00:18:12,258 --> 00:18:13,801
-Thank you!
-Appreciate it.

359
00:18:13,884 --> 00:18:15,928
-[receptionist] Enjoy.
-Thank you so much.

360
00:18:16,012 --> 00:18:17,471
-Have a good day.
-Hello.

361
00:18:17,555 --> 00:18:19,849
-Nice to meet you.
-Welcome to the Historic Strater Hotel.

362
00:18:19,932 --> 00:18:21,017
-Hello.
-Hello.

363
00:18:21,100 --> 00:18:23,603
-Nice to meet you.
-Can I show you a couple of hiding spots?

364
00:18:23,686 --> 00:18:25,688
-What? What spot?
-Yeah.

365
00:18:25,771 --> 00:18:26,939
[Genta] Is it a box?

366
00:18:27,732 --> 00:18:28,899
[Genta] What is…

367
00:18:28,983 --> 00:18:31,152
[Shime, Machu, Genta exclaim]

368
00:18:31,235 --> 00:18:32,653
-What is this?
-[guide] We got some candy.

369
00:18:32,737 --> 00:18:34,530
-[Machu] Candy!
-[Genta] Can I get this one?

370
00:18:34,614 --> 00:18:35,740
-[guide] Yes.
-[Genta] Really?

371
00:18:35,823 --> 00:18:37,199
-[Machu] Really?
-[Genta] Yeah.

372
00:18:37,283 --> 00:18:38,534
-[Shime] What a kind man.
-Thank you.

373
00:18:38,618 --> 00:18:40,161
-Thank you.
-[guide] You're very welcome.

374
00:18:40,244 --> 00:18:41,078
This is something else.

375
00:18:41,162 --> 00:18:42,913
I've never seen such
a trick in all my life.

376
00:18:42,997 --> 00:18:44,582
[in English] Why… Why make it?

377
00:18:44,665 --> 00:18:47,543
Well, back in the day, people didn't
trust banks or banks weren't available.

378
00:18:47,627 --> 00:18:49,503
So they would build hiding spots
to hide their valuables

379
00:18:49,587 --> 00:18:51,756
like keys and money
and jewelry and stuff like that.

380
00:18:51,839 --> 00:18:53,674
-Mmm.
-So these are just three examples

381
00:18:53,758 --> 00:18:56,719
of what we put in to show people
what they would've done back in the day.

382
00:18:56,802 --> 00:18:58,179
[in Japanese] Oh, I see.

383
00:18:58,846 --> 00:19:01,724
What a fun little gimmick.

384
00:19:01,807 --> 00:19:03,100
Yeah, it's like we're in a movie.

385
00:19:03,184 --> 00:19:04,393
And let me show you another secret.

386
00:19:04,477 --> 00:19:06,020
[in English] Oh, okay.

387
00:19:06,103 --> 00:19:07,772
-[in Japanese] Wow.
-It's amazing.

388
00:19:07,855 --> 00:19:09,732
-[in English] Where?
-[in Japanese] What? There's more?

389
00:19:09,815 --> 00:19:11,275
[Shime, Machu, Genta exclaim]

390
00:19:11,359 --> 00:19:12,360
Incredible.

391
00:19:12,443 --> 00:19:15,196
[guide] So these were done with
little miniatures as you can see.

392
00:19:15,279 --> 00:19:17,907
Um, a lot of the front desk folks
and a lot of the maintenance guys

393
00:19:17,990 --> 00:19:20,826
got together and decided
to make this into another spare bedroom.

394
00:19:20,910 --> 00:19:21,911
-[Genta] Whoa.
-Just for fun,

395
00:19:21,994 --> 00:19:23,412
-but mainly for the little kids.
-Yeah.

396
00:19:23,496 --> 00:19:24,872
[in Japanese] Is this your room, Machu?

397
00:19:24,955 --> 00:19:26,040
[in English] Is this my room?

398
00:19:26,123 --> 00:19:27,375
-Yes, it is.
-Okay. [laughs]

399
00:19:27,458 --> 00:19:29,043
This is where
you'll be checking into tonight.

400
00:19:29,126 --> 00:19:30,378
-See you tomorrow, Machu.
-Off I go.

401
00:19:30,461 --> 00:19:31,462
See you tomorrow.

402
00:19:32,088 --> 00:19:33,089
[Genta laughs]

403
00:19:33,172 --> 00:19:35,091
[in Japanese]
You'll break it! You'll break it!

404
00:19:35,174 --> 00:19:36,842
You don't fit. Really, you don't!

405
00:19:36,926 --> 00:19:38,844
-Oh, I bet children get a kick out of it.
-For sure.

406
00:19:38,928 --> 00:19:40,763
This place is fun
for the entire family, kids included.

407
00:19:40,846 --> 00:19:42,056
There's something for everyone here.

408
00:19:42,139 --> 00:19:44,100
-Amazing!
-[in English] I love it. I love it.

409
00:19:44,183 --> 00:19:45,559
-[in Japanese] That's super fun.
-Amazing.

410
00:19:45,643 --> 00:19:46,644
-[guide] Yep.
-Yeah.

411
00:19:46,727 --> 00:19:48,354
-[in Japanese] Thank you.
-All right, let me show you

412
00:19:48,437 --> 00:19:49,772
another one right over here.

413
00:19:50,773 --> 00:19:52,400
This is my third secret hiding spot.

414
00:19:52,483 --> 00:19:53,526
[Shime, Machu, Genta exclaim]

415
00:19:53,609 --> 00:19:54,985
[in Japanese] Here, too?

416
00:19:55,569 --> 00:19:56,737
[in English] Isn't that cool?

417
00:19:56,821 --> 00:20:01,409
Now again, they would've put money, keys,
jewelry, even a gun.

418
00:20:01,492 --> 00:20:03,452
-Wow. That is amazing.
-Interesting.

419
00:20:03,536 --> 00:20:04,620
-Isn't that cool?
-Yeah, cool.

420
00:20:04,704 --> 00:20:05,955
Yeah, it's so cool.

421
00:20:06,580 --> 00:20:08,708
[in Japanese] Wow.

422
00:20:14,630 --> 00:20:16,841
[Noel] The name "Durango" originates

423
00:20:16,924 --> 00:20:19,093
from the Basque word "urango",

424
00:20:19,176 --> 00:20:20,845
which means, "the city of water."

425
00:20:20,928 --> 00:20:22,138
[water flowing]

426
00:20:23,639 --> 00:20:27,810
True to its name, a river flows
through the heart of the city,

427
00:20:28,394 --> 00:20:30,604
and along its riverbanks
is a nature retreat.

428
00:20:31,731 --> 00:20:36,444
This river's pristine, abundant waters
have bestowed a boon to the city.

429
00:20:38,154 --> 00:20:39,780
And this boon is…

430
00:20:42,825 --> 00:20:44,493
Genta's favorite drink,

431
00:20:45,077 --> 00:20:46,579
beer.

432
00:20:47,079 --> 00:20:49,415
-[in English] Local brewery.
-Local brewery.

433
00:20:49,498 --> 00:20:50,875
[♪ rock music plays]

434
00:20:50,958 --> 00:20:52,126
Look, everyone's chuggin' it down.

435
00:20:52,209 --> 00:20:53,878
-True.
-Sure looks good.

436
00:20:53,961 --> 00:20:56,964
I'm jealous.
Nothing quite beats day drinking.

437
00:20:57,047 --> 00:20:58,632
Yeah, while the sun's still out.

438
00:21:01,969 --> 00:21:02,970
[in English] Oh, yeah!

439
00:21:03,053 --> 00:21:04,930
Oh, yeah!

440
00:21:05,014 --> 00:21:08,017
[narrator, in Japanese] The three of them
first head to a beer brewery.

441
00:21:08,601 --> 00:21:09,643
[in English] Yeah!

442
00:21:12,313 --> 00:21:13,773
-[in Japanese] It's huge!
-Huge.

443
00:21:14,732 --> 00:21:15,733
What the heck?

444
00:21:17,485 --> 00:21:18,903
[♪ music continues]

445
00:21:20,529 --> 00:21:22,531
[Machu, in English] Ah, local brewery?

446
00:21:24,074 --> 00:21:25,367
[in Japanese] Isn't it massive?

447
00:21:26,660 --> 00:21:27,870
[♪ music stops]

448
00:21:27,953 --> 00:21:30,706
-The entire place smells like beer.
-Look at these humongous vats.

449
00:21:30,790 --> 00:21:32,166
[machinery whirring]

450
00:21:33,250 --> 00:21:34,251
[in English] I'm Genta.

451
00:21:34,335 --> 00:21:35,586
Nice to meet you. I'm Machu.

452
00:21:35,669 --> 00:21:36,879
Hi, nice to meet you.

453
00:21:36,962 --> 00:21:37,963
-I'm Shime.
-Great.

454
00:21:38,047 --> 00:21:40,216
Nice to meet all of you.
You guys want to learn about brewing?

455
00:21:40,299 --> 00:21:41,675
-Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Right.

456
00:21:41,759 --> 00:21:42,760
Let's head up top.

457
00:21:42,843 --> 00:21:43,844
[Genta] Wow.

458
00:21:44,386 --> 00:21:45,387
This way? Oh.

459
00:21:45,471 --> 00:21:47,056
-[in Japanese] Wow. I'm not even kidding.
-Right?

460
00:21:52,520 --> 00:21:53,938
[brewery guide, in English] So, today

461
00:21:54,021 --> 00:21:58,234
we are brewing a batch of
our flagship IPA, Modus Hoperandi.

462
00:21:58,943 --> 00:22:00,528
This is our mash tun here.

463
00:22:00,611 --> 00:22:01,737
[Genta] Ooh.

464
00:22:01,821 --> 00:22:03,948
[brewery guide]
So, what's in there is this grain here.

465
00:22:04,031 --> 00:22:05,032
Uh-huh. Uh-huh.

466
00:22:05,115 --> 00:22:07,952
[brewery guide] And we turn the starch
inside the grain into sugar…

467
00:22:08,035 --> 00:22:11,330
-Sugar.
-…that will be later turned into alcohol.

468
00:22:11,413 --> 00:22:12,581
[Shime] Uh-huh.

469
00:22:13,624 --> 00:22:15,167
-[brewery guide] You want to eat some?
-[Shime] Yeah.

470
00:22:15,793 --> 00:22:17,211
-For real? Are you sure?
-Wow.

471
00:22:17,294 --> 00:22:19,088
-[Machu] Really?
-Yeah.

472
00:22:19,171 --> 00:22:20,589
[in Japanese] Are they edible?

473
00:22:20,673 --> 00:22:21,882
It's good.

474
00:22:23,384 --> 00:22:25,553
-Yum.
-It's a little bitter.

475
00:22:25,636 --> 00:22:26,637
Right.

476
00:22:27,972 --> 00:22:29,181
Don't they remind you of peanuts?

477
00:22:29,265 --> 00:22:30,266
-Yep.
-It's tasty.

478
00:22:30,349 --> 00:22:32,518
-[in English] Like peanuts?
-Like peanuts.

479
00:22:32,601 --> 00:22:36,814
Mm-hmm. So, after this,
we rinse all that through

480
00:22:36,897 --> 00:22:39,066
and it goes into
this kettle behind you here.

481
00:22:39,149 --> 00:22:41,402
[in Japanese] Oh, wow. Ooh.

482
